  "answer_text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola did originally contain cocaine.** \n\nThe beverage\u2019s connection to the drug is a matter of historical fact, though the recipe has changed dramatically since the drink was first invented. \n\n### The Origins of Coca-Cola\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1885 by John Stith Pemberton, an Atlanta-based pharmacist who was searching for a cure for his own morphine addiction (which he had developed after being wounded in the Civil War). \n\nHe formulated a drink that combined two primary active ingredients, which gave the beverage its name:\n* **\"Coca\":** Extracts from the **coca leaf**, which contained the active alkaloid cocaine.\n* **\"Cola\":** Extracts from the **kola nut**, which provided a high dose of caffeine.\n\nPemberton originally marketed the drink as a patent medicine and \"brain tonic\" that could cure headaches, relieve fatigue, and soothe anxiety. \n\n### How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nWhile early Coca-Cola did contain cocaine, it was not a massive recreational dose. During the late 1800s, it is estimated that a single glass of Coca-Cola contained about **9 milligrams of cocaine**. For perspective, a typical modern recreational \"line\" of cocaine contains roughly 50 to 75 milligrams. Still, 9 milligrams was more than enough to give drinkers a noticeable, stimulant-driven buzz.\n\n### When and Why Was It Removed?\nIn the late 19th and early 20th centuries, public perception of cocaine shifted dramatically. Once viewed as a medical miracle, it was increasingly recognized as a highly addictive and dangerous substance. \n\nPressured by changing public opinion and impending regulations, the Coca-Cola Company (then led by Asa Griggs Candler) began phasing cocaine out of the recipe. \n* **In 1903**, the company officially switched from using fresh coca leaves to **\"spent\" (decocainized) coca leaves** for flavoring. \n* **By 1929**, scientists perfected the extraction process, ensuring that the coca leaf flavor extract added to Coca-Cola was entirely, 100% free of even trace amounts of cocaine.\n\n### The Modern Connection: Coca-Cola Today\nFascinatingly, Coca-Cola **still uses coca leaves** in its secret recipe today for flavoring, but the drink itself is entirely cocaine-free. \n\nBecause importing coca leaves is highly illegal under international drug laws, the U.S. government has a special arrangement to make this possible:\n1. A specialty chemical company in New Jersey called **Stepan Company** holds the exclusive federal license to import dried coca leaves from South America (primarily Peru).\n2. At their facility, Stepan Company processes the leaves to **extract the cocaine**, leaving behind a cocaine-free flavoring extract.\n3. This flavor extract is sent to Coca-Cola to be used in the soda.\n4. The extracted cocaine is not destroyed. It is legally sold to **Mallinckrodt**, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into *cocaine hydrochloride*\u2014a prescription topical anesthetic used by doctors and dentists to numb the ears, nose, and throat during minor surgeries.",
